{"strongs": "H0842", "word": "אֲשֵׁרָה", "translit": "a.she.rah", "root": "אשף", "morph": "N:N-F-T", "pos": "proper name, feminine", "gloss": "Ashera", "definition": "אֲשֵׁרָה , אֲשֵׁירָה 2Kgs 17:16 proper name, feminine Ashera (Assyrian proper name, feminine Aš-ra-tu , with sign for deity, in Canaanitish proper name Abad-Ašratum, servant of A. Schr ZA 1888, 363 , compare Wkl & Abel Thontafelfund see El Amarna ii. No. 77, 1. 9, & Sayce RP2. ii. 67, iii. 71 ; on derivatives compare Assyrian aširat , adjective feminine gracious , COT Gloss ): ** see now also GFM EB ASHERAH Dr Deut 16:21 Allen DB ASHERAH , all doubtful as to Canaanite goddess Asherah; question left open by Zim KAT 3. 436 ff. (on Semitic goddess Aširtu-Ašratu Id. ib, 432 ff. ); but see Jerem AT im Licht d. Alten Orients 207 (name of goddess Aširat in letter found at Taanach by Sellin (1902-3) and Id. ib. 37. 237 ) (Oppenheim's find at Ras el-`Ain in Mesopotamia, stone shaft with veiled head as top, supposed to identify post with goddess ; if 2Kgs 23:7 refers to draped Asherim [see on text Benz Bur], this even more plausible), Id. ib. 23, 208 f. 236 . On pictorial representations of Asherah -symbol see WHWard AJSL xix.l (Oct.1902) . — usually with the article: probably \\ \\ a. a Canaanitish goddess of fortune & happiness; having prophets 1Kgs 18:19 , an image 1Kgs 15:13 = 2Chr 15:16 ; 2Kgs 21:7 , sacred vessels 2Kgs 23:4 , houses v 2Kgs 23:7 . \\ \\ b. a symbol of this goddess, a sacred tree or pole set up near an altar 1Kgs 16:33 ; 2Kgs 13:6 ; 2Kgs 17:16 ; 2Kgs 18:4 ; 2Kgs 21:3 ; 2Kgs 23:6 ; 2Kgs 23:15 prohibited Deut 16:1 burnt by Gideon Judg 6:25 ; Judg 6:26 ; Judg 6:28 ; Judg 6:30 . Plural אֲשֵׁרוֺת \\ \\ a. the goddess Judg 3:7 (probably error for עַשְׁתָּרֹת ᵑ9 ). \\ \\ b. sacred trees or poles 2Chr 19:3 ; 2Chr 33:3 elsewhere אֲשֵׁרִים id. Isa 27:9 + 12 t.; suffix Mic 5:14 + 5 t.; — Exod 34:13 ( J ) Deut 7:5 ; Deut 12:3 ; Isa 17:8 ; Isa 27:9 ; Jer 17:20 ; Mic 5:13 ; 1Kgs 14:15 ; 1Kgs 14:23 ; 2Kgs 17:10 ; 2Kgs 23:14 ; 2Chr 14:2 ; 2Chr 17:6 ; 2Chr 24:18 ; 2Chr 31:1 ; 2Chr 33:19 ; 2Chr 34:3 ; 2Chr 34:4 ; 2Chr 34:7 . — (compare also Sta ZAW 1881, 344 f. RS Semitic i. 171 f., 175 n. We H 235 , who think ׳ א only the sacred pole.)", "definition_html": "<h1><entry onclick=\"bdbid('BDB843')\">BDB843</entry> [<entry onclick=\"sn('H842')\">H842</entry>]</h1><div class=\"navigation\"><entry onclick=\"bdbid('BDB842')\">BDB842</entry> | BIBLICAL HEBREW | <entry onclick=\"bdbid('BDB844')\">BDB844</entry></div><p> <bdbheb>אֲשֵׁרָה</bdbheb>, <bdbheb>אֲשֵׁירָה</bdbheb> <ref ref=\"2Kgs 17:16\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"17\" vBegin=\"16\" cEnd=\"17\" vEnd=\"16\" onclick=\"bcv(12,17,16)\">2Kgs 17:16</ref> <b>proper name, feminine</b> <highlightword>Ashera</highlightword> (Assyrian proper name, feminine <highlight>Aš-ra-tu</highlight>, with sign for deity, in Canaanitish proper name <highlight>Abad-Ašratum, servant of A.</highlight> <lookup onclick=\"bdbabb('Schr')\">Schr<sup>ZA 1888, 363</sup></lookup>, compare <lookup onclick=\"bdbabb('Wkl')\">Wkl</lookup> &amp; Abel<sup>Thontafelfund</sup> see El Amarna ii. No. 77, 1. 9, &amp; <lookup onclick=\"bdbabb('Say')\">Sayce<sup>RP2. ii. 67, iii. 71</sup></lookup>; on derivatives compare Assyrian <transliteration><highlight>aširat</highlight></transliteration>, adjective feminine <highlight>gracious</highlight>, <lookup onclick=\"bdbabb('COT')\">COT<sup>Gloss</sup></lookup>): <div class=\"remarks\"><b>**</b>see now also GFM<sup>EB ASHERAH</sup> Dr<sup><ref ref=\"Deut 16:21\" b=\"5\" cBegin=\"16\" vBegin=\"21\" cEnd=\"16\" vEnd=\"21\" onclick=\"bcv(5,16,21)\">Deut 16:21</ref></sup> Allen<sup>DB ASHERAH</sup>, all doubtful as to Canaanite goddess Asherah; question left open by Zim<sup>KAT 3. 436 ff.</sup> (on Semitic goddess <transliteration><highlight>Aširtu-Ašratu</highlight></transliteration> Id.<sup>ib, 432 ff.</sup>); but see Jerem<sup>AT im Licht d. Alten Orients 207</sup> (name of goddess <transliteration><highlight>Aširat</highlight></transliteration> in letter found at Taanach by Sellin (1902-3) and Id.<sup>ib. 37. 237</sup>) (Oppenheim's find at Ras el-`Ain in Mesopotamia, stone shaft with veiled head as top, supposed to identify <highlight>post</highlight> with <highlight>goddess</highlight>; if <ref ref=\"2Kgs 23:7\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"23\" vBegin=\"7\" cEnd=\"23\" vEnd=\"7\" onclick=\"bcv(12,23,7)\">2Kgs 23:7</ref> refers to draped Asherim [see on text Benz Bur], this even more plausible), Id.<sup>ib. 23, 208 f. 236</sup>. On pictorial representations of <highlight>Asherah</highlight>-symbol see WHWard<sup>AJSL xix.l (Oct.1902)</sup>. — usually with the article: probably <br>\\&emsp;\\&emsp;<b>a.</b> a Canaanitish goddess of fortune &amp; happiness; having prophets <ref ref=\"1Kgs 18:19\" b=\"11\" cBegin=\"18\" vBegin=\"19\" cEnd=\"18\" vEnd=\"19\" onclick=\"bcv(11,18,19)\">1Kgs 18:19</ref>, an image <ref ref=\"1Kgs 15:13\" b=\"11\" cBegin=\"15\" vBegin=\"13\" cEnd=\"15\" vEnd=\"13\" onclick=\"bcv(11,15,13)\">1Kgs 15:13</ref> = <ref ref=\"2Chr 15:16\" b=\"14\" cBegin=\"15\" vBegin=\"16\" cEnd=\"15\" vEnd=\"16\" onclick=\"bcv(14,15,16)\">2Chr 15:16</ref>; <ref ref=\"2Kgs 21:7\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"21\" vBegin=\"7\" cEnd=\"21\" vEnd=\"7\" onclick=\"bcv(12,21,7)\">2Kgs 21:7</ref>, sacred vessels <ref ref=\"2Kgs 23:4\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"23\" vBegin=\"4\" cEnd=\"23\" vEnd=\"4\" onclick=\"bcv(12,23,4)\">2Kgs 23:4</ref>, houses v<ref ref=\"2Kgs 23:7\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"23\" vBegin=\"7\" cEnd=\"23\" vEnd=\"7\" onclick=\"bcv(12,23,7)\">2Kgs 23:7</ref>. <br>\\&emsp;\\&emsp;<b>b.</b> a symbol of this goddess, a <highlight>sacred tree</highlight> or <highlight>pole</highlight> set up near an altar <ref ref=\"1Kgs 16:33\" b=\"11\" cBegin=\"16\" vBegin=\"33\" cEnd=\"16\" vEnd=\"33\" onclick=\"bcv(11,16,33)\">1Kgs 16:33</ref>; <ref ref=\"2Kgs 13:6\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"13\" vBegin=\"6\" cEnd=\"13\" vEnd=\"6\" onclick=\"bcv(12,13,6)\">2Kgs 13:6</ref>; <ref ref=\"2Kgs 17:16\" b=\"12\" cBegin=\"17\" vBegin=\"16\" c